From a27f2ad2917555201a9a97ee52b1ee23bee93798 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Bj=C3=B6rn=20Strausmann?= <projects@strausmann.ch>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 14:22:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(webrtc): limit NACK retransmissions

Reading RTCP (#2486) activates pion's NACK responder, which
resends every requested packet still in its 1024-packet history. Its only
rate brake depends on the GCC target bitrate (responder_interceptor.go:213),
which go2rtc never registers, so one peer could make go2rtc send ~1000x its
own NACK traffic (measured: 1.2 MB sent, ~30 MB resent during a 1 s flood).

Add a NACK limiter interceptor that removes NACK entries beyond a budget
before the responder handles them:
- resend bytes per 500 ms window <= bytes the stream itself sent in that
  window, clamped to [32 KiB, 1 MiB] (2 MiB/s ceiling per stream)
- at most 5 resends per sequence number

The default interceptors are now registered one by one (identical to
webrtc.RegisterDefaultInterceptors in pion/webrtc v4.2.25 interceptor.go:33-74)
with the limiter first. Order matters: interceptor.Chain passes incoming RTCP
to interceptors in registry order (pion/interceptor v0.1.51 chain.go:19-25).
Registered after the responder, the limiter only works by racing the
responder's resend goroutine (responder_interceptor.go:115); the race
detector reports it.

Tests: flood budget, per-sequence limit; TestConnectionRetransmitsNack
still repairs a single loss. Removing the limiter turns both new tests red.
